# PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS] Contents of this Part

## SECTION I SCOPE AND INTERPRETATION

## Scope

## 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (a) Section II sets out the mode of application for claims under this Part;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (b) Section III relates to the procedure for making claims to controlled goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (c) Section IV relates to the procedure for making claims against executed goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (d) Section V relates to the procedure for a debtor making a claim to exempt goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (e) Section VI relates to the powers of the court hearing any application under this Part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (2) The rules in this Part apply where—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (a) a person makes an application to the court claiming that goods of which control has been taken belong to that person and not to the debtor;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (b) a person makes an application to the court claiming that goods, money or chattels taken or intended to be taken under a writ of execution or the proceeds or value of such goods or chattels belong to that person and not to the debtor; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.1 (1) This Part contains rules about claims on controlled goods and executed goods as follows—] (c) a debtor, whose goods have been made subject to an enforcement power under an enactment, writ or warrant of control or have been taken or are intended to be taken under a writ of execution, claims that such goods or any of them are exempt goods. ## Interpretation

## 85.2

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (1) In this Part—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (a) ‘the Act’ means the Tribunals, Courts and Enforcement Act 2007 1 ;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (b) ‘claim to controlled goods’ is a claim made under paragraph 60(1) of Schedule 12;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (c) ‘a claim to exempt goods’ means a claim by a debtor whose goods have been subject to an enforcement power under an enactment, writ or warrant of control or the right to execute conferred by a writ of execution, that such goods are exempt goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (d) ‘claimant to controlled goods’ means any person making a claim to controlled goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (e) ‘claimant to executed goods’ means any person making a claim to executed goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (f) ‘the court’ has the meaning given in paragraph 60(8) of Schedule 12 2 , in respect of a claim to controlled goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (g) ‘debtor’s home court’ means the Central Office or District Registry of the High Court or the County Court hearing centre serving the address where the debtor resides or carries on business;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (h) ‘enforcement agent’ has the meaning given in paragraph 2(1) of Schedule 12;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (i) ‘enforcement officer’ means an individual who is authorised to act as an enforcement officer under the Courts Act 2003 3 ;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (j) ‘executed goods’ means goods subject to a writ of execution;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (k) ‘exempt goods’ —

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (i) in respect of controlled goods has the meaning given in paragraph 3(1) of Schedule 12 and defined in regulations 4 and 5 of the TCG Regulations 4 ; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (ii) in respect of executed goods has the meaning given in paragraph 9(3) of Schedule 7 to the Courts Act 2003;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (l) ‘goods subject to enforcement’ refers to either controlled goods or executed goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (m) ‘relevant enforcement officer’ means—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (i) in relation to a writ of execution which is directed to a single enforcement officer, that officer; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (ii) in relation to a writ of execution which is directed to two or more enforcement officers, the officer to whom the writ is allocated;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (n) ‘required payments’ has the meaning given in paragraph 60(4) of Schedule 12 5 ;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (o) ‘Schedule 12’ means Schedule 12 to the Act 6 ;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (p) ‘TCG Regulations’ means the Taking Control of Goods Regulations 2013;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (q) ‘warrant of control’ is to be construed in accordance with section 62(4) of the Act;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (r) ‘writ of control’ is to be construed in accordance with section 62(4) of the Act;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (s) ‘writ of execution’ includes—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (i) a writ of possession;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (ii) a writ of delivery;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (iii) a writ of sequestration;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (iv) writs relating to ecclesiastical property, namely—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (aa) a writ of fieri facias de bonis ecclesiasticis;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (bb) a writ of sequestrari de bonis ecclesiasticis,

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] and any further writ in aid of any such writs, but does not include a writ of control;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (t) the following words or phrases have the meaning given in paragraph 1 of Schedule 12, in respect of a claim to controlled goods—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (i) ‘creditor’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (ii) ‘debt’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (iii) ‘debtor’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (iv) ‘enforcement power’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (u) the following words or phrases have the meaning given in paragraph 3(1) of Schedule 12 7 —

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (i) ‘control’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (ii) ‘controlled goods’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (iii) ‘co-owner’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (iv) ‘disposal’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (v) ‘interest’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (vi) ‘money’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (vi) ‘premises’;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.2] (vii) ‘securities’. ## SECTION II MODE OF APPLICATION FOR CLAIMS UNDER THIS PART

## Mode of application for claims under this Part

## 85.3 Any claim under this Part must be made by an application in accordance with Part 23. ## SECTION III PROCEDURE FOR MAKING A CLAIM TO CONTROLLED GOODS

## Procedure for making a claim to controlled goods

## 85.4

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (1) Any person making a claim under paragraph 60(1) of Schedule 12 must, as soon as practicable but in any event within 7 days of the goods being removed under the exercise of an enforcement power, give notice in writing of their claim to the enforcement agent who has taken control of the goods (‘the notice of claim to controlled goods’) and must include in such notice—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (a) their full name and address, and confirmation that such address is their address for service;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (b) a list of all those goods in respect of which they make such a claim; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (c) the grounds of their claim in respect of each item. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (2) On receipt of a notice of claim to controlled goods which complies with paragraph (1) the enforcement agent must within 3 days give notice of such claim to—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (a) the creditor; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (b) any other person making a claim to the controlled goods under paragraph (1) (‘any other claimant to the controlled goods’);

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (3) The creditor, and any other claimant to the controlled goods, must, within 7 days after receiving the notice of claim to controlled goods, give notice in writing to the enforcement agent informing them whether the claim to controlled goods is admitted or disputed in whole or in part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (4) The enforcement agent must notify the claimant to the controlled goods in writing within 3 days of receiving the notice in paragraph (3) whether the claim to controlled goods is admitted or disputed in whole or in part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (5) A creditor who gives notice in accordance with paragraph (3) admitting a claim to controlled goods is not liable to the enforcement agent for any fees and expenses incurred by the enforcement agent after receipt of that notice by the enforcement agent. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (6) If an enforcement agent receives a notice from a creditor under paragraph (3) admitting a claim to controlled goods the following applies—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (a) the enforcement power ceases to be exercisable in respect of such controlled goods; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (b) as soon as reasonably practicable the enforcement agent must make the goods available for collection by the claimant to controlled goods if they have been removed from where they were found. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (7) Where the creditor, or any other claimant to controlled goods to whom a notice of claim to controlled goods was given, fails, within the period mentioned in paragraph (3), to give the required notice, the enforcement agent may seek—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (a) the directions of the court by way of an application; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.4] (b) an order preventing the bringing of any claim against them for, or in respect of, their having taken control of any of the goods or having failed so to do. ## Procedure for making a claim to controlled goods where the claim is disputed

## 85.5

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (1) Where a creditor, or any other claimant to controlled goods to whom a notice of claim to controlled goods was given, gives notice under rule 85.4(3) that the claim to controlled goods, or any part of it, is disputed, and wishes to maintain their claim to the controlled goods, the following procedure will apply. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (2) The claimant to controlled goods must make an application which must be supported by—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (a) a witness statement—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (i) specifying any money;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (ii) describing any goods claimed; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (iii) setting out the grounds upon which their claim to the controlled goods is based; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (b) copies of any supporting documents that will assist the court to determine the claim. (3) In the High Court the claimant to controlled goods must serve the application notice and supporting witness statements and exhibits on—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (a) the creditor;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (b) any other claimant to controlled goods of whom the claimant to controlled goods is aware; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (c) the enforcement agent. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (4) In the County Court when the application is made the claimant to controlled goods must provide to the court the addresses for service of—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (a) the creditor;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (b) any other claimant to controlled goods of whom the claimant to controlled goods is aware; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (c) the enforcement agent,

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (‘the respondents’), and the court will serve the application notice and any supporting witness statement and exhibits on the respondents. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (5) An application under paragraph (2) must be made to the court which issued the writ or warrant conferring power to take control of the controlled goods, or, if the power was conferred under an enactment, to the debtor’s home court. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (6) The claimant to controlled goods must make the required payments on issue of the application in accordance with paragraph 60(4)(a) of Schedule 12 8 , unless such claimant seeks a direction from the court that the required payment be a proportion of the value of the goods, in which case they must seek such a direction immediately after issue of the application, on notice to the creditor and to the enforcement agent. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (7) The application notice will be referred to a Master or District Judge. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (8) On receipt of an application for a claim to controlled goods, the Master or District Judge may—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (a) give directions for further evidence from any party;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (b) list a hearing to give directions;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (c) list a hearing of the application;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (d) determine the amount of the required payments, make directions or list a hearing to determine any issue relating to the amount of the required payments or the value of the controlled goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (e) stay, or dismiss, the application if the required payments have not been made;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (f) make directions for the retention, sale or disposal of the controlled goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.5] (g) give directions for determination of any issue raised by a claim to controlled goods. ## SECTION IV PROCEDURE FOR MAKING A CLAIM AGAINST EXECUTED GOODS

## Procedure for making a claim against executed goods

## 85.6

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (1) A claimant to executed goods must, as soon as practicable but in any event within 7 days of the goods being removed by the enforcement officer, give notice in writing of their claim to the relevant enforcement officer (“the notice of claim to executed goods”) and must include in such notice—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (a) their full name and address, and confirmation that such address is their address for service;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (b) a list of all those goods in respect of which they make such a claim; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (c) the grounds of their claim in respect of each item. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (2) On receipt of a notice of claim to executed goods which complies with paragraph (1) the enforcement officer must within 3 days give notice of such claim to—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (a) the creditor; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (b) any other person making a claim to the executed goods under paragraph (1) (‘any other claimant to the executed goods’). [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (3) The creditor, and any other claimant to executed goods, must, within 7 days after receiving the notice of claim to the executed goods, give notice in writing to the enforcement officer informing them whether the claim to the executed goods is admitted or disputed in whole or in part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (4) The enforcement officer must notify the claimant to executed goods in writing within 3 days of receiving the notice in paragraph (3) whether the claim to executed goods is admitted or disputed in whole or in part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (5) A creditor who gives notice in accordance with paragraph (3) admitting a claim to executed goods is not liable to the enforcement officer for any fees and expenses incurred by the enforcement officer after receipt of that notice by the enforcement officer. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (6) If an enforcement officer receives a notice from a creditor under paragraph (3) admitting a claim to executed goods the following applies—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (a) the writ of execution ceases to be exercisable in respect of such executed goods; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (b) as soon as reasonably practicable the enforcement officer must make the goods available for collection by the claimant to executed goods if the enforcement officer has removed the goods from where they were found. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (7) Where the creditor, or any other claimant to executed goods to whom a notice of claim to executed goods was given, fails, within the period mentioned in paragraph (3), to give the required notice, the enforcement officer may seek—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (a) the directions of the court by way of an application; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (b) an order preventing the bringing of any claim against them for, or in respect of, the seizure of the executed goods or their having failed so to do. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.6] (8) An application under paragraph (7) must be made to the court which issued the writ of execution. ## Procedure for making a claim to executed goods where the claim is disputed

## 85.7

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (1) Where a creditor, or any other claimant to executed goods to whom a notice of claim to executed goods was given, gives notice under rule 85.6(3) that the claim to executed goods, or any part of it, is disputed, and wishes to maintain their claim, the following procedure will apply. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (2) The claimant to executed goods must make an application by application notice which must be supported by—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (a) a witness statement—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (i) specifying any money;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (ii) describing any goods claimed; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (iii) setting out the grounds upon which the claim to the executed goods is based; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (b) copies of any supporting documents that will assist the court to determine the claim. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (3) The claimant to executed goods must serve the application notice and supporting witness statements and exhibits on—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (a) the creditor;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (b) any other claimant to the executed goods of whom they are aware; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (c) the relevant enforcement officer. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (4) An application under paragraph (2) must be made to the court which issued the writ of execution. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (5) The application notice will be referred to a Master or District Judge of a District Registry. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (6) On receipt of an application for a claim to executed goods, the Master or District Judge may—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (a) give directions for further evidence from any party;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (b) list a hearing to give directions;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (c) list a hearing of the application;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (d) make directions for the retention, sale or disposal of the executed goods; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (e) give directions for determination of any issue raised by a claim to executed goods. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.7] (Rule 83.3(11) provides that the validity of a writ of execution is automatically extended following an application under paragraph (2) until 12 months from the conclusion of the application proceedings.)

## SECTION V PROCEDURE FOR A DEBTOR MAKING A CLAIM TO EXEMPT GOODS

## Procedure for a debtor making a claim to exempt goods

## 85.8

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (1) A debtor making a claim to exempt goods must, as soon as practicable and in any event within 7 days of the removal of the goods, give notice in writing of the claim to exempt goods (“notice of claim to exempt goods”) to the enforcement agent who has taken control of the goods or relevant enforcement officer and must include in such notice—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (a) their full name and address and that address is their address for service;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (b) a list of all those goods in respect of which they make such a claim; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (c) the grounds of the claim in respect of each item. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (2) On receipt of a notice of claim to exempt goods, the enforcement agent or relevant enforcement officer must within 3 days give notice of such claim to—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (a) the creditor; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (b) any other person making a claim under rule 85.4 or 85.6 to the goods subject to enforcement (‘any other claimant to the goods subject to enforcement’). [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (3) The creditor, and any other claimant to the goods subject to enforcement, must, within 7 days after receiving the notice of claim to exempt goods, give notice in writing to the enforcement agent or relevant enforcement officer informing them whether the claim to exempt goods is admitted or disputed in whole or in part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (4) The enforcement agent or relevant enforcement officer must notify the debtor in writing within 3 days of receiving the notice in paragraph (3) whether the claim to exempt goods is admitted or disputed in whole or in part. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (5) A creditor who gives notice in accordance with paragraph (3) admitting a claim to controlled goods or to executed goods is not liable to the enforcement agent or officer for any fees and expenses incurred by the enforcement agent or officer after receipt of that notice by the enforcement agent or officer. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (6) If an enforcement agent or relevant enforcement officer receives a notice from a creditor and from any other claimant to the goods subject to enforcement under paragraph (3) admitting a claim to exempt goods the following applies—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (a) the enforcement power ceases to be exercisable, and the right to execute conferred by any writ of execution ceases to have effect, in respect of such exempt goods;

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (b) as soon as reasonably practicable the enforcement agent or relevant enforcement officer must make the goods available for collection by the debtor if the enforcement agent or officer has removed them from where they were found. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (7) Where the creditor, or any other claimant to the goods subject to enforcement to whom notice of claim to exempt goods was given, fails, within the period mentioned in paragraph (3), to give the required notice, the enforcement agent or relevant enforcement officer may seek—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (a) the directions of the court by way of an application; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (b) an order preventing the bringing of any claim against them for, or in respect of, their having taken control of or seized by execution any of the goods or their having failed to do so. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.8] (8) An application under paragraph (7) must be made to the court which issued the writ or warrant conferring power to take control of controlled goods, or the writ of execution or, if the power to take control of controlled goods was conferred under an enactment, to the County Court hearing centre which is the debtor’s home court. ## Procedure for making a claim to exempt goods where the claim is disputed

## 85.9

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.9] (1) Where a creditor, or any other claimant to goods subject to enforcement to whom notice of a claim to exempt goods was given, gives notice under rule 85.8 that the claim to exempt goods, or any part of it, is disputed, and wishes to maintain their claim on the goods subject to enforcement, the following procedure will apply. [PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.9] (2) The debtor must make an application within 7 days of receiving the notice under rule 85.8(3) which must be supported by—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.9] (a) a witness statement—

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.9] (i) describing any goods to which a claim to exempt goods is made; and

[PART 85 – CLAIMS ON CONTROLLED GOODS AND EXECUTED GOODS > 85.9] (ii) setting out the grounds upon which such claim is based; and

